WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.340
KWEICHOW MOUTAI:
How do you make $300 billion&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:02.340 --> 00:00:09.000
by selling liquor? Well, the answer is by getting&nbsp;
the CCP or the Chinese Communist Party addicted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:16.080
to it and charging them a fortune. This probably&nbsp;
sounds quite daunting and borderline impossible,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.080 --> 00:00:21.060
but that’s precisely what a company&nbsp;
called Kweichow Moutai has managed to do.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.600 --> 00:00:27.660
That name probably doesn’t ring a bell but&nbsp;
Kweichow Moutai is actually the 25th largest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:27.660 --> 00:00:34.260
company in the world with a market cap of $307&nbsp;
billion. For perspective, that’s more than Coca&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:34.260 --> 00:00:39.300
Cola and Pepsi which means that Kweichow Moutai&nbsp;
is the largest beverage company in the world.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.300 --> 00:00:45.540
In fact, they’re almost the largest food company&nbsp;
in the world as well, only beaten out by Nestle.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:45.540 --> 00:00:53.220
They’re also the 2nd largest Chinese company&nbsp;
only beaten out by Tencent. This is no doubt&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:53.220 --> 00:00:59.760
quite impressive, but when you take a look at how&nbsp;
much Moutai costs, it all makes sense. A single&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.760 --> 00:01:08.280
bottle of Moutai costs anywhere between $300 to&nbsp;
$44,000 depending on the age. For a 500 ml bottle,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:08.280 --> 00:01:16.800
this translates to 60 cents to 88 dollars per ml.&nbsp;
For perspective, even human blood only costs 39&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:24.360
cents per ml. And that’s just normal moutai. It’s&nbsp;
not uncommon to hear about vintage Moutai selling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:24.360 --> 00:01:30.480
for more than a million dollars. This has allowed&nbsp;
Kweichow to grow to $20 billion in revenue and $13&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:30.480 --> 00:01:37.800
billion in net profit every single year with&nbsp;
a net margin of over 53% but it wasn’t always&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.800 --> 00:01:43.740
like this. In fact, just 20 years ago, the entire&nbsp;
company was barely pulling in $200 million worth&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:43.740 --> 00:01:50.040
of revenue and $75 million worth of profit. This&nbsp;
means that within a matter of 20 years, Kweichow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:50.040 --> 00:01:57.420
has managed to grow 100 to 200X which is very much&nbsp;
reflected in the stock price which has run up as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:57.420 --> 00:02:05.220
much as 290X. Even if you bought Google at IPO,&nbsp;
you would’ve only gotten a 50X return. So, how&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:05.220 --> 00:02:10.260
Kweichow Moutai went from a boutique distillery&nbsp;
to being the world’s largest beverage company with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:10.260 --> 00:02:16.140
a drink that tastes like rotten cabbage? Well,&nbsp;
the answer is a bit of deception, some powerful&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:22.920
connections, and a lot of brilliant marketing.&nbsp;
So, here is the insane story of Kweichow Moutai.

00:02:22.920 --> 00:02:25.440
THE FALSE ORIGIN:&nbsp;

00:02:25.440 --> 00:02:36.480
Taking a look back, Kweichow is by no means a&nbsp;
heritage company. In fact, the current version&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:36.480 --> 00:02:43.440
wasn’t even created till the end of 1999 but the&nbsp;
same cannot be said about Moutai which according&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:50.580
to some sources dates back over 2000 years to 135&nbsp;
BC. This was during the rise of the Han Dynasty&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:50.580 --> 00:02:56.820
not to be confused with the Huns from Mulan. The&nbsp;
Han Dynasty is one of the strongest dynasties in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.820 --> 00:03:02.580
China’s history having ruled over much of modern&nbsp;
day China for over 4 centuries. Many of the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:02.580 --> 00:03:08.520
traditions and cultures that we now associate with&nbsp;
China today can be traced back to the Hans. So,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:08.520 --> 00:03:14.040
it wouldn’t be surprising if Moutai was also&nbsp;
popularized by the Hans. The story goes that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:14.040 --> 00:03:19.920
a diplomat from the Western Han dynasty named Tang&nbsp;
Meng would travel to an independent kingdom named&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:19.920 --> 00:03:26.220
Nanyue back in 135 BC. During this trip, Tang&nbsp;
would apparently enjoy a drink which he brought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:26.220 --> 00:03:33.180
back to the Han Dynasty. According to legends and&nbsp;
Kweichow’s own history page, this is the origin of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:33.180 --> 00:03:40.020
Moutai. But, what if I told you that all of that&nbsp;
was a facade, a legend popularized by Kweichow&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.020 --> 00:03:45.420
for marketing purposes. You see, according to&nbsp;
historical scholars and records, there was indeed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:45.420 --> 00:03:52.140
a man named Tang Meng who traveled to Nanyue back&nbsp;
in 135 BC. There’s even evidence that he did enjoy&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:52.140 --> 00:04:00.240
a drink there but this drink was not Moutai, it&nbsp;
was Jujiang. So, the reality is that Moutai is no&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:00.240 --> 00:04:06.540
where near that ancient but it’s in Kweichow’s&nbsp;
best interest to convince people that it is. A&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.940
lot of the appeal of Moutai is that its what the&nbsp;
greatest Chinese people of history drank and its&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.940 --> 00:04:18.360
the drink of China. This ideology and Chinese&nbsp;
pride is what really sells the drink because&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:18.360 --> 00:04:25.920
the drink itself is vile and putrid and I can’t&nbsp;
understate that. In fact, here are some reviews.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:26.700 --> 00:04:32.220
“Imagine rotten cabbage, ethyl alcohol,&nbsp;
and paint thinner, blended and strained.”&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:32.220 --> 00:04:38.640
“The first time I tried Moutai it tasted like&nbsp;
engine oil.” “I’ve never met anybody, even at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:38.640 --> 00:04:44.460
the heights of alcoholic derangement, prepared to&nbsp;
admit that they actually liked the taste.” None&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:44.460 --> 00:04:50.160
of these reviews are that surprising though when&nbsp;
you consider that Moutai is 53% alcohol. So yeah,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:50.160 --> 00:04:55.800
Moutai is a terrible tasting drink that has&nbsp;
nothing to do with the Han dynasty. The real&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:55.800 --> 00:05:00.600
roots of Moutai actually go back to the much&nbsp;
more modern Qing dynasty who were in power&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:07.380
between 1644 and 1911. Sometime during this&nbsp;
period, some northern Chinese distillers would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:07.380 --> 00:05:13.500
apparently introduce some advanced techniques&nbsp;
to create a distinctive type of Baiju known as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:13.500 --> 00:05:20.400
Moutai. But again, this is mostly just fluff.&nbsp;
The reality is that the invention of Moutai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:20.400 --> 00:05:27.840
was unceremonies and largely unknown because it&nbsp;
wasn’t really an invention to begin with. These&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:27.840 --> 00:05:32.220
northern Chinese distillers were simply making&nbsp;
classic liquor using the climate and ingredients&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:32.220 --> 00:05:38.160
that they had access to. It just so happened that&nbsp;
this climate and these ingredients gave the liquor&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:38.160 --> 00:05:45.480
a unique taste which has since become the iconic&nbsp;
taste of Moutai. So, while Kweichow likes to claim&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:45.480 --> 00:05:51.180
that Moutai is some sort of magic juice with a&nbsp;
2000 year history, Moutai is actually just some&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.180 --> 00:05:57.660
random local drink with a terrible taste. But,&nbsp;
none of this would hold back Moutai in the 1900s.

00:05:57.660 --> 00:06:02.040
CHINESE PRIDE:&nbsp;

00:06:02.040 --> 00:06:09.360
Like with many popular products, the&nbsp;
popularity of Moutai has more to do with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.360 --> 00:06:15.960
optics than intrinsic value. And the optics&nbsp;
of Moutai can be traced back to 1915 to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:15.960 --> 00:06:21.960
Panama Pacific International Exposition. The&nbsp;
exposition was held in San Francisco and is one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:21.960 --> 00:06:26.280
of the most monumental events in American&nbsp;
history. 9 years before the exposition,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:26.280 --> 00:06:33.960
San Franciso experienced the worst earthquake in&nbsp;
history estimated to be a magnitude of 7.9. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:39.060
exposition was designed to show that not just&nbsp;
San Francisco but all of America was willing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.060 --> 00:06:44.220
to fight through and recover from anything. As&nbsp;
such, this exposition would be marketed like&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:44.220 --> 00:06:51.240
never before and attracted a whopping 18 million&nbsp;
people. The exposition showcased some of the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:51.240 --> 00:06:57.180
advanced tech of the time like America’s first&nbsp;
transcontinental telephone call. In one corner&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:57.180 --> 00:07:03.300
of this grand exposition, there an exhibit for&nbsp;
Chinese Moutai. While no where near as impressive&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.300 --> 00:07:08.640
as a transcontinental telephone call, Moutai was&nbsp;
no doubt a heavy weight when it came to liquor.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.360 --> 00:07:14.820
The aroma and taste was something that the&nbsp;
rest of the world had never experienced. This&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.820 --> 00:07:19.980
not only made them popular at the exposition&nbsp;
but they would straight up win a gold medal,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.980 --> 00:07:26.340
establishing China’s presence on the world stage.&nbsp;
This was the first time that Moutai was linked to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:26.340 --> 00:07:32.940
Chinese pride but it was by no means the last. The&nbsp;
event that would certify Moutai as a hood classic&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:32.940 --> 00:07:39.900
was the Chinese Civil War between 1927 and 1949.&nbsp;
This war was fought between the Republic of China&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.900 --> 00:07:44.160
backed by the United States and the Chinese&nbsp;
Communist Party backed by the Soviet Union.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:44.760 --> 00:07:50.040
The army of the CCP is referred to as the&nbsp;
People’s Liberation Army and the story goes&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:56.280
that Moutai is what led this army to victory.&nbsp;
Apparently, Red Army soldiers drank Moutai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:56.280 --> 00:08:00.000
to lift their spirits and disinfect their&nbsp;
wounds during the battle of Chishui River.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:06.780
This battle turned out to be the turning point of&nbsp;
the bloody 22 year long war leading to the CCP’s&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.780 --> 00:08:13.680
victory. And as this battle become legendary&nbsp;
so did Moutai. Now, to be clear, we don’t even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:13.680 --> 00:08:19.620
know if these soldiers used Moutai but that’s&nbsp;
the story that’s propagated by Kweichow Moutai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:19.620 --> 00:08:25.680
and the CCP alike. After the war, the CCP would&nbsp;
double down on this image, advertising that Moutai&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:25.680 --> 00:08:31.260
was the favorite drink of the Chinese leader Mao&nbsp;
Zedong. But, it wasn’t just Mao who enjoyed this&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.260 --> 00:08:37.320
drink. Pretty soon, Moutai became quite popular&nbsp;
with foreign leaders as well. Likely the most&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:37.320 --> 00:08:42.900
notable instance of this was with President&nbsp;
Richard Nixon in 1972. Thanks to the Cold War,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:42.900 --> 00:08:49.200
China and the US were basically sworn enemies up&nbsp;
until this point. So, this meeting between Richard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:49.200 --> 00:08:55.860
Nixon and Chinese leader Zhou Enlai was very much&nbsp;
unprecedented. And to celebrate this monumental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.860 --> 00:09:02.220
event in Beijing, the CCP would of course serve&nbsp;
Moutai. We don’t know what President Nixon thought&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:02.220 --> 00:09:07.320
about the drink but his secretary of state,&nbsp;
Henry Kissinger, was much more vocal. He said&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.320 --> 00:09:13.740
​​“I think if we drink enough Moutai we can solve&nbsp;
anything.” This event cemented Moutai as not just&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:13.740 --> 00:09:18.510
the drink of the CCP but as the drink of the elite&nbsp;
leading us to the modern day Kweichow Moutai.

00:09:18.510 --> 00:09:19.660
AN EMPIRE:&nbsp;

00:09:22.560 --> 00:09:33.960
Over the next few decades, Moutai transitioned&nbsp;
from being a pride thing to being a status symbol.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:33.960 --> 00:09:39.060
It was the drink of millionaires and billionaires&nbsp;
and if you were hosting someone important,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:39.060 --> 00:09:46.080
serving them Moutai was the standard but there&nbsp;
was no standard brand. While all Moutai came from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:52.800
the city of Moutai, there were several brands&nbsp;
that you could choose from until 1999. In 1999,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:52.800 --> 00:09:57.960
the CCP would consolidate all of the most&nbsp;
notable Moutai distilleries into one state&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.960 --> 00:10:04.560
owned enterprise called Kweichow Moutai. While&nbsp;
this created a monopoly, it wasn’t a very big&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:04.560 --> 00:10:10.800
deal. It’s not like Apple and Google were merging.&nbsp;
Rather, it was a bunch of local distilleries who&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:10.800 --> 00:10:17.820
barely had a combined revenue of $200 million.&nbsp;
But then, how did such a small operation become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.820 --> 00:10:24.180
the world’s largest beverage company? Well,&nbsp;
the answer isn’t rocket science. One look at&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:24.180 --> 00:10:31.500
China’s GDP graph will explain a lot. Back in&nbsp;
1999, China’s GDP stood at $1 trillion. Today,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.500 --> 00:10:39.480
China’s GDP stands at $17.7 trillion meaning&nbsp;
that China’s GDP has grown 17X. For perspective,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.480 --> 00:10:46.440
America’s GDP has only grown about 2.5X during&nbsp;
the same time period. So, Kweichow Moutai has&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.440 --> 00:10:52.200
acted as a sort of index for Chinese wealth&nbsp;
and the power of the CCP. In fact, the vast&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:52.200 --> 00:10:58.080
majority of Moutai is consumed by the government&nbsp;
officials. In 2012, it was estimated that half of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:58.080 --> 00:11:03.660
all sales came from government related customers.&nbsp;
President Xi has tried to fight against on this.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:09.240
One of the first things he did after coming to&nbsp;
power in 2012 was crackdown on the spending of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.240 --> 00:11:16.080
CCP officials. This did momentarily reduce the&nbsp;
price of Moutai but it has since recovered which&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:16.080 --> 00:11:23.820
just goes to show powerful the brand really is&nbsp;
but it’s not all good news. For one, Moutai is&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.820 --> 00:11:30.060
only relevant within China. In fact, 97% of all&nbsp;
of Kweichow’s revenue comes from China alone.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:30.720 --> 00:11:35.520
While westerners often try the drink when&nbsp;
visiting China, they rarely pick it up as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:35.520 --> 00:11:42.120
habit because it tastes like “drinking liquid&nbsp;
razor blades”, not to mention the price. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:42.120 --> 00:11:48.180
it’s not just westerners who are shying away&nbsp;
from Moutai. China’s youth isn’t so fond of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:48.180 --> 00:11:52.140
the drink either primarily because of the&nbsp;
taste and association with older generations.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:52.860 --> 00:11:59.880
Currently, the core demographic for the liquor is&nbsp;
40 to 60 year old men. So, only time will tell if&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.880 --> 00:12:05.700
Moutai will survive through generations but as&nbsp;
for the imminent future it’s not going anywhere.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:06.240 --> 00:12:13.980
In fact, in late 2022, Kweichow briefly overtook&nbsp;
Tencent to become China’s largest company period.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:13.980 --> 00:12:18.240
So, there’s really no question that&nbsp;
Kweichow Moutai has basically become&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:18.240 --> 00:12:24.300
the expensive alcoholic Coca Cola of China,&nbsp;
and that won’t be changing anytime soon.

00:12:24.300 --> 00:12:31.260
A BRAND:&nbsp;

00:12:31.260 --> 00:12:36.180
In the end, it’s easy to dismiss Kweichow&nbsp;
Moutai as some sort of bougie company that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:36.180 --> 00:12:41.580
sells ridiculously priced liquor to China's&nbsp;
elite. But, when we peel back the layers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:41.580 --> 00:12:47.400
it’s clear that Kweichow Moutai and more&nbsp;
specifically Moutai itself is much more than that.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:47.400 --> 00:12:54.540
It’s really a testament to the power of branding.&nbsp;
Objectively speaking, Moutai has virtually nothing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.540 --> 00:13:00.540
going for it. It’s a byproduct of random&nbsp;
Chinese city’s climate and resources. But,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:00.540 --> 00:13:06.480
through the power of narrative, story telling,&nbsp;
and branding, Kweichow Moutai has made Moutai into&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:06.480 --> 00:13:12.420
a Chinese icon. They were able to tie together&nbsp;
random events from over 2000 years ago to modern&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:12.420 --> 00:13:18.480
events that hit close to home like the Chinese&nbsp;
Civil War and Mao Zedong. And while there may&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:18.480 --> 00:13:24.180
be a longer term trend away from Moutai just like&nbsp;
there is with soda, Moutai is by no means going&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:24.180 --> 00:13:32.100
anywhere but up anytime soon. And that is how you&nbsp;
make $300 billion by getting the CCP drunk. Would&nbsp;&nbsp;

